.TH sort 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
sort \- sort and merge text files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sort
[\fB\-bcdfimnru\fR] [\fB\-t\fI x\fR] [\fB+\fIbeg\fR [\fB\-\fIend\fR]] [\fB\-o\fI outfile\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sort
sorts the lines of the named files together and writes the result to
standard output.  With no
.IR file ,
or with
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Ignore leading blanks when comparing.
.TP
.B \-c
Check only: report whether the input is already sorted, and sort
nothing.
.TP
.B \-d
Dictionary order: compare only letters, digits and blanks.
.TP
.B \-f
Fold case, so that upper and lower case sort together.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore characters outside the printable ASCII range.
.TP
.B \-m
Merge: the inputs are already sorted.
.TP
.B \-n
Compare leading numeric strings by value.  Implies
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write to
.I outfile
rather than standard output.  It may be one of the input files.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the order.
.TP
.BI \-t x
Use
.I x
as the field separator instead of blanks.
.TP
.B \-u
Output only the first of a run of equal lines.
.SH SORT KEYS
Keys are given in the old positional form.
.BI + beg
starts the key at field
.I beg
counting from zero, and
.BI \- end
ends it before field
.IR end .
The flags
.BR b ,
.BR d ,
.BR f ,
.BR i ,
.B n
and
.B r
may be appended to a key so that they apply only to it.
.PP
There is no
.B \-k
option.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# sort names
# sort -nr sizes
# sort -t: +2n -3 /etc/passwd     by uid
# sort -u -o list list
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/sort.*
Sorted runs, while a large sort is in progress.
.SH NOTES
.B sort
holds 40K of lines at a time.  Anything larger is written out as sorted
runs in
.B /tmp
and merged at the end, up to 26 of them \- so a big sort needs room on
the filesystem holding
.BR /tmp ,
and fails without it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uniq (1),
.BR comm (1),
.BR join (1)
